---
import referenceDocument from "../../../../public/studies/mercury-2026-11-12/references.json";
import framing from "../../../components/studies/rock-planets/art-029-framing.json";
import RockArt029 from "../../../components/studies/rock-planets/RockArt029.svelte";
import RockComparison from "../../../components/studies/rock-planets/RockComparison.svelte";
import RockFlight015 from "../../../components/studies/rock-planets/RockFlight015.svelte";
import RockLight030 from "../../../components/studies/rock-planets/RockLight030.svelte";
import RockSize026 from "../../../components/studies/rock-planets/RockSize026.svelte";
import RockSurface016 from "../../../components/studies/rock-planets/RockSurface016.svelte";
import RockTerrain025 from "../../../components/studies/rock-planets/RockTerrain025.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const references = referenceDocument.references;
const astronomy = referenceDocument.astronomy;
const concepts = [
  {
    id: "mineral" as const,
    title: "A / Mineral mosaic",
    cue: "Mineral colour carries the material: slate and ochre deposits interrupt a dark stone body.",
    reference: "02.2 · Mercury’s enhanced colour",
    limitation:
      "The strong colour boundaries may suggest exposed stone slabs rather than planetary geology.",
  },
  {
    id: "highlands" as const,
    title: "B / Ancient highlands",
    cue: "A broad dark plain and worn bright highlands carry the world through landform and value.",
    reference: "02.1 · Escape Velocity’s form hierarchy; 02.2 · lunar plains",
    limitation: "Restrained colour may lose material identity beside the Sun.",
  },
  {
    id: "impact" as const,
    title: "C / Impact palimpsest",
    cue: "Broken terrain boundaries and a sparse pale impact trace carry the surface’s history.",
    reference: "02.2 · Mercury’s bright rays and muted rock",
    limitation:
      "Impact traces may become decoration, or disappear at the smallest diameter.",
  },
];

function imageFraming(id: keyof typeof framing) {
  const frame = framing[id];
  return `width: ${(100 * frame.width) / frame.side}%; height: ${(100 * frame.height) / frame.side}%; left: ${(-100 * frame.x) / frame.side}%; top: ${(-100 * frame.y) / frame.side}%;`;
}

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "mercury-2026-11-12");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / A first planetary language</h2>
    <p>
      Today’s planets are flat coloured discs with pale outlines. Airless rock
      planets inside stellar charging fields begin the game’s planetary visual
      language: barren worlds exposed to severe sunlight, with material
      character and boundaries readable against stellar effects. The result must
      work across physical sizes and viewing scales. Mercury is a reference and
      the first practice-world application, not the limit of this planet kind.
    </p>
    <p>
      Open: surface structure, colour, light and shadow, detail across scales,
      and nearby HUD treatments such as orbit-line colour. Examples must compare
      several sizes together and state the production star profile, body radii
      and camera scale. Comparison values are fixtures, not accepted tuning or
      limits on the family. Mercury’s
      <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mercury/facts/"
        >cratered rock and thin exosphere</a
      >
      anchor the airless reading. “Scorched” is an impression; molten or
      luminous ground is not a requirement.
    </p>
    <p>
      Procedural drawing and readability before realism guide the work. Orbits,
      collisions and gravitational pull provide context without fixing every
      rule; the size–mass relationship remains unresolved. The flat-disc visual
      contract is open to replacement. Mechanical questions arising from visual
      examples need bounded investigation, not a general physics redesign. For
      now, airless rock is the only intended planet kind inside charging fields.
      Other planet kinds, new heat mechanics and a general lighting overhaul are
      outside this cycle. The answer changes the shared rock-planet drawing
      across modes.
      <a href="/lab/reviews/mercury-2026-11-12#references"
        >Period-art comparisons</a
      >
      inform it; practice’s reconstructed home system, Mercury’s name and
      eventual HUD label remain application context.
    </p>
    <aside aria-label="Question test">
      <p>
        Question test rerun · 12 November 2026: One subject — Pass; Grounded —
        Pass with findings; Consequence — Pass. The reviewer found comparison
        conditions imprecise and the opening too long, with no additional
        finding on the mechanics boundary. The opening was shortened and the
        comparison requirement clarified afterwards; these edits were not
        retested.
        <a href="/lab/reviews/mercury-2026-11-12">Full result and responses</a>.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>02 / Exploration</h2>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-1">02.1 / What carries a planet at a glance?</summary>
      <p>
        Four games offer contrasting ways to convey a planetary surface and
        form. These reduced, attributed excerpts support visual commentary. Full
        frames are retained at no more than 320 × 240 pixels, without crops or
        recolouring; each source links to its context. Their art remains
        reference material.
      </p>
      <div class="reference-board">
        {references.map((reference) => (
          <figure>
            <h4>{reference.title}</h4>
            <p class="reference-context">{reference.year}</p>
            <a
              class="reference-image"
              href={reference.source}
              aria-label={`Source for ${reference.title}`}
            >
              <img
                src={reference.image}
                width={reference.width}
                height={reference.height}
                alt={reference.alt}
                loading="lazy"
              />
            </a>
            <figcaption>
              <p class="reference-credit">
                {reference.credit}.{" "}
                <a href={reference.source}>Source and context ↗</a>
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Visible:</strong> {reference.observation}
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>For our rock planets:</strong> {reference.implication}
              </p>
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>
      <h3>Directions to put into our own examples</h3>
      <p>
        A broad light-and-shadow structure can carry the globe before surface
        detail carries its material. Two useful alternatives follow: sparse
        crater marks over large rock planes, and denser mottling within a
        simpler shaded sphere. Compare both with the same untextured silhouette,
        across several body sizes beside the practice G7 Sun. The question is
        how little surface information can still feel like exposed rock while
        keeping the boundary clear.
      </p>
      <p>
        These are provisional directions to reconsider alongside the
        astronomical references in 02.2. The images use different cameras and
        scales; reducing a screenshot also shrinks its planet, so this board
        cannot establish equal-scale readability. None demonstrates our
        charging-field overlap. The SC2 image shows surface exploration, not a
        Super Melee planet. The Earth-like examples show useful form cues while
        making the atmosphere and material differences explicit.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-2">
        02.2 / Rock seen through light and colour processing
      </summary>
      <p>
        Astronomy supplies both the material reference and ways of seeing it.
        These NASA figures distinguish a restrained visible-colour
        interpretation from algorithmic enhancement of measured differences. The
        processing is part of each caption; colour alone cannot establish heat,
        atmosphere or emitted light. The Moon contributes an airless-rock
        comparison alongside Mercury, rather than another planet inside a
        charging field.
      </p>
      <div class="reference-board">
        {astronomy.map((reference) => (
          <figure>
            <h4>{reference.title}</h4>
            <p class="reference-context">{reference.year}</p>
            <a
              class="reference-image"
              href={reference.source}
              aria-label={`Source for ${reference.title}`}
            >
              <img
                src={reference.image}
                width={reference.width}
                height={reference.height}
                alt={reference.alt}
                loading="lazy"
              />
            </a>
            <figcaption>
              <p class="reference-credit">
                {reference.credit}.{" "}
                <a href={reference.source}>NASA image and method ↗</a>
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Image method:</strong> {reference.observation}
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>For our rock planets:</strong> {reference.implication}
              </p>
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p>
        Together, these references suggest separating three choices in our own
        examples: the rock structure, its illumination, and its colour
        treatment. Craters, plains and rays can remain consistent while a
        restrained or enhanced palette changes. That would let 02.3 compare
        natural-looking and more expressive rock without making every
        close-to-star planet molten. This is a proposed experiment, not an
        accepted palette or renderer.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-3">02.3 / One rock, three colour readings</summary>
      <p>
        The first translation into the game separates material from colour. Each
        column uses the same crater positions, broad terrain regions,
        directional light and camera. Only the three material colours change.
        This tests whether restrained stone, warm rock or enhanced mineral
        colour best holds the airless reading beside the Sun, before we tune
        detail or choose a palette.
      </p>
      <div class="colour-comparison">
        <article>
          <h4>A / Restrained stone</h4>
          <p>Grey-brown regions; shape and relief do most of the work.</p>
          <RockComparison treatment="stone" client:load />
        </article>
        <article>
          <h4>B / Warm rock</h4>
          <p>The same regions interpreted through ochre and warm stone.</p>
          <RockComparison treatment="warm" client:load />
        </article>
        <article>
          <h4>C / Enhanced minerals</h4>
          <p>Blue-violet and gold distinguish the same material regions.</p>
          <RockComparison treatment="enhanced" client:load />
        </article>
      </div>
      <p>
        Reference: Practice G7, the current non-ranked yellow-family Sun, using
        its production body and field painters at 1.8 seconds. Ranked currently
        retains the legacy Sun; the yellow-family model is planned for ranked
        outside Playground in 0.7.0. That transition is context, not delivered
        by this study. The collection field is shown active. Three rock bodies
        have radii 18, 30 and 45 u at centre distance 255 u, all wholly inside
        the roughly 324 u charging reach; the field view uses 0.7 CSS pixels per
        world unit. The enlarged comparisons use radius 30 at 1.8 pixels per
        unit.
      </p>
      <p>
        Study-local sketches: a deterministic material map, no borrowed image
        textures, atmosphere or emission. Lighting follows the direction to the
        star with a provisional night-side floor. Colour is an artistic
        interpretation, not a calibrated reconstruction of NASA filters.
        Position and time are frozen for comparison; there is no flight, orbit
        or mass experiment here. The current disc beside each enlarged sketch
        comes from the production planet painter. Surface, lighting and scale
        choices are provisional; no direction has been selected.
      </p>
      <h4>Findings / Field context before colour</h4>
      <p>
        The comparison establishes three requirements for this case. The Sun’s
        gravity, charging and danger boundaries must be drawn so that the
        planet’s placement can be understood. A mercurial rock planet’s entire
        surface must remain outside the solar danger zone. At least part of its
        surface must lie inside the solar charging zone; the whole body may lie
        inside it. “Mercurial” is a working name for this environmental case,
        not a settled planet taxonomy or a restriction to the named Mercury.
      </p>
      <p>
        The step is useful for exposing the surrounding fields and placement
        requirements, but it does not establish a colour preference. These
        sketches introduce a way of drawing planets that is substantially
        different from the game’s other objects, so colour cannot yet be judged
        independently of form and shading. Smooth, strongly volumetric shading
        raises a question about how much dimensionality belongs here; a more
        three-dimensional planet has not been ruled out. Neither the material
        map nor its shading treatment is accepted by the usefulness of this
        comparison.
      </p>
      <p>
        The next proposed investigation is the drawing language itself: hold
        colour and field context steady while comparing a graphic disc with
        sparse relief, a globe built from a few flat planes, and a smoothly
        shaded globe. Judge them beside the same production Sun and a real ship
        at flight scale, with enlarged views for examining construction. Colour
        can be revisited once the planet has a convincing place in the game’s
        visual language.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-4">
        02.4 / Finding: planets need a material language
      </summary>
      <p>
        Comparing a graphic disc, a globe of hull-like planes and a smoothly
        shaded globe beside the Wasp exposed the wrong premise. Borrowing the
        construction of ships and fittings did not bring the planets closer to
        the game and astronomy references in 02.1 and 02.2. Their surface
        character remained six large crater symbols over broad patches. The
        three drawings are retired; the useful result is that a planet needs its
        own geological material and rendering language. Colour remains
        undecided, and dimensionality itself has not been rejected.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <a href="/lab/studies/plasma-light-2026-11-11#mark">plasma study</a>
        records a related distinction: chains of diamonds, rings and strokes
        failed to convey turbulent flame. A cached luminous stamp supplied its
        character; an overly blocky sprite was also rejected before the
        <a href="/lab/studies/plasma-light-2026-11-11#luminous"
          >luminous treatment</a
        >
        emerged. Hi-Fi Lo-Fi supports rich, expressive images made with
        inexpensive rendering. Hull planes are a language for manufactured
        objects, not a rule every natural body or effect must inherit.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-5">02.5 / A surface made of terrain</summary>
      <p>
        This step builds one materially richer planet before returning to colour
        alternatives. Many overlapping impacts, rough ground, a darker plain and
        bright ejecta replace the six-symbol surface. The terrain is mapped onto
        a sphere, so features compress toward the limb and relief participates
        in the light. A restrained warm-grey palette keeps the question on the
        surface.
      </p>
      <RockTerrain025 client:load />
      <p>
        The left view reuses the Practice G7 Sun, its gravity, charging and
        danger markings, and the production starter Wasp. Body radii 18, 30 and
        45 u sit at distances 220, 260 and 330 u. Each entire surface stays
        outside the 100 u danger reach; the outer body intersects the roughly
        324 u charging boundary, while the smaller two are wholly inside. The
        camera uses 0.9 px/u. The top-right detail uses radius 45 at 3 px/u; the
        lower row shows three seeded surfaces at 1.1 px/u. Ship drawings keep
        the production flight scale of 0.65 within each view. Time is frozen at
        1.8 seconds.
      </p>
      <p>
        The study-local terrain is baked once into a 192 × 192 image using
        three-dimensional noise, 180 seeded impacts plus one fixed rayed impact,
        and a relief-derived normal. Thirty-two luminance steps and subtle
        dithering give the raster a limited tonal vocabulary without drawing
        large pixel cells. No source photograph is sampled. The same terrain
        seed appears at all three sizes and in the enlarged view; two additional
        crater seeds test reuse. The light and night-side visibility floor are
        provisional. This static scene does not establish motion, rotation,
        performance in play, or an accepted art direction.
      </p>
      <h4>Finding / The geological direction is closer</h4>
      <p>
        Terrain supplies a more convincing planetary character than the earlier
        symbolic surfaces. The example remains too realistic and detailed, its
        dark side falls too far into the void, and its dithered finish reads too
        much like pixel art. The next interpretation should keep a material-rich
        globe while simplifying the surface and making the unlit side readable.
        NASA Mercury, SC2 Mercury and this procedural example provide a range to
        work between; none alone defines the intended result.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-6">
        02.6 / Finding: smoothness does not establish representation
      </summary>
      <p>
        A version with fewer impacts, smoother colour and a brighter dark side
        was no improvement. Both terrain examples were closer than the ship-like
        constructions, but the difference did not establish a better planet. The
        softer variant is retired. The useful direction is a corporeal,
        game-like representation; a procedural map, a smooth finish and a final
        palette all remain open. This differs from plasma’s turbulence question.
      </p>
      <h4>Accepted principle / Suggest a world</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Accepted art-direction principle, 12 November 2026:</strong>
        deliberately compose the image to suggest a world rather than trying to
        reproduce one. This distillation has been adopted in
        <code>docs/design.md</code>. The study is assessed as already successful
        for clarifying that intent, independently of the eventual planet result.
        The study remains open: no planet appearance, procedural map requirement
        or delivery scope is accepted by that assessment.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-7">
        02.7 / Finding: crater symbols distort the scale
      </summary>
      <p>
        Composed light and material remain useful, but the oversized, naive
        crater forms invite the pixel-art reading. The problem is more specific
        than smoothness, fidelity or the rendering medium. Craters were made to
        announce “rock planet” at a scale where they may not need to be
        individually visible. The large crater landmarks are retired. This
        finding does not establish that every rocky planet needs a procedural
        map, nor that relief must become visible at a particular zoom.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-8">
        02.8 / Finding: removing relief does not establish rock
      </summary>
      <p>
        A crater-free sphere with broad, smoothly varying colour read as a
        photorealistic gas giant. Removing oversized crater symbols did not
        establish a rocky material. Crater visibility remains open; the surface
        needs a composed material identity before its rendering method is
        chosen.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-9">02.9 / Three composed rock worlds</summary>
      <p>
        These original image concepts translate three qualities from 02.1 and
        02.2: mineral colour, broad landforms and sparse impact traces. Each
        uses a round body lit from the left, a readable shadow and a dry
        surface. This comparison tests their whole impression at flight size; it
        does not select an image-production technique or a palette for the game.
      </p>
      <div class="art-directions">
        {concepts.map((concept) => (
          <figure>
            <h4>{concept.title}</h4>
            <p>{concept.cue}</p>
            <div class="flight-sprites">
              {[32, 54, 81].map((diameter) => (
                <div>
                  <span
                    class="planet-frame"
                    style={`--diameter: ${diameter}px`}
                  >
                    <img
                      src={`/studies/mercury-2026-11-12/art-029-${concept.id}.png`}
                      style={imageFraming(concept.id)}
                      alt={`${concept.title}, ${diameter}-pixel body`}
                    />
                  </span>
                  <small>{diameter} px</small>
                </div>
              ))}
            </div>
            <figcaption>
              <p>{concept.reference}.</p>
              <p>{concept.limitation}</p>
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>
      <RockArt029 client:load />
      <p>
        Matched context: production Practice G7 Sun and field markings, starter
        Wasp, 0.9 px/u. Each radius-30 planet is 230 world units from the Sun’s
        centre, entirely outside danger and inside charging. The same image is
        rescaled for each diameter; these are static art concepts, with no
        rotation or gameplay changes. Source framing is normalized to the
        visible body.
      </p>
      <details>
        <summary>Inspect the authored images at 162-pixel diameter</summary>
        <div class="art-directions">
          {concepts.map((concept) => (
            <figure>
              <h4>{concept.title}</h4>
              <span class="planet-frame" style="--diameter: 162px">
                <img
                  src={`/studies/mercury-2026-11-12/art-029-${concept.id}.png`}
                  style={imageFraming(concept.id)}
                  alt={`${concept.title}, enlarged for inspection`}
                />
              </span>
            </figure>
          ))}
        </div>
      </details>
      <p>
        Created with the built-in image generator as study-only raster concepts;
        the references were interpreted, not sampled into these images. Matching
        lighting is an authoring instruction, not a calibrated shared shader.
        All three improve on the earlier examples: concrete, relatively
        believable surfaces make the disc feel like a planetary body. Detail and
        shading can support the intended representation; “too photorealistic”
        was too broad a diagnosis to guide further work. This establishes a
        useful quality, without choosing a variant, final fidelity or production
        technique.
        <a href="/studies/mercury-2026-11-12/art-029-prompts.json"
          >Prompts and provenance</a
        >.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-10">02.10 / A solid world facing its Sun</summary>
      <p>
        Keep the believable stone surface and compare three light treatments:
        fixed left-side shade, Sun-facing shade drawn into the body, and the
        same Sun-facing light field through the production LFX hard-light blend.
        Surface markings remain in place. The highlands image is a convenient
        specimen, not the selected planet design.
      </p>
      <RockLight030 client:load />
      <p>
        All three use one neutrally illuminated image derived from 02.9 B. This
        edit preserves the large composition approximately; local painted relief
        remains, so it is not measured albedo or a recovered normal map. A and B
        multiply the material by a broad sphere-light field. C keeps the
        material on World and paints that field, clipped to the body, on LFX.
        Mid-grey is neutral in hard-light; darker and lighter values change
        contrast and highlights differently from multiplying the surface colour.
        B and C receive the same field, so their response is the comparison.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each panel uses the real FlightLayers stack and its production blending;
        FX is empty and LFX is populated only in C. Sun, fields and Wasp use
        production drawing. Fixture bodies have radius 45, centre distance 220
        and camera scale 0.6 px/u: 54-pixel diameters, wholly outside danger and
        inside charging. Lower views are 81 pixels. The illustrative light field
        runs from 0.29 to 0.65; doubling it gives the multiplication treatment a
        58% shadow floor. Surface orientation is fixed. These are static
        examples, without overlapping transient effects or a performance
        measurement.
      </p>
      <p>
        LFX’s hard-light character may be useful for reflected surface light as
        well as the live emitters it currently carries. This comparison tests
        that possibility before deciding layer ownership. Body masks, overlap
        with other effects and drawing cost would need investigation before
        adoption.
        <strong
          >Preferred direction: 02.10 C, Sun-facing LFX hard-light.</strong
        >
        Its washed, semi-realistic appearance gives the surface a convincing
        planetary presence. This selects the light treatment for further study;
        exact shadow depth, colour response, material and delivery remain open.
        <a href="/studies/mercury-2026-11-12/art-030-provenance.json"
          >Material-edit prompt and provenance</a
        >.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-11">
        02.11 / A little more shadow, a little Sun colour
      </summary>
      <p>
        Retain the preferred LFX treatment and compare two small changes. A
        reproduces 02.10 C. B deepens only the shadow, C colours only the light
        facing the Sun, and D combines them. Material, light direction, body
        size, positions and peak neutral light level stay the same. The four
        panels separate shadow depth from colour response.
      </p>
      <RockLight030 round="tuning" client:load />
      <p>
        The neutral light field’s minimum changes from 0.29 to 0.25 while its
        maximum stays 0.65: the darkest material response falls from 58% to 50%.
        This is a modest test, not a return to a black night side. All light is
        still composited through real LFX hard-light. Scene diameters remain 54
        pixels; lower examples are 81 pixels, with the same 02.10 fixtures.
      </p>
      <p>
        The colour hypothesis takes the production G7 body colour from
        <code>yellowStarPalette</code>. A restrained 12% chromatic contribution
        affects only the light field above neutral grey; its mean channel value
        stays unchanged. Shadow colour and the underlying rock image are
        untouched. This is an art-directed response, not physical colour
        rendering. It tests a steady yellow Sun before adding variable corona or
        flare colours; no new star palette, animated flare response or
        production light system is introduced by this step.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-12">
        02.12 / Can generated stone keep the planetary character?
      </summary>
      <p>
        The retained image is the visual target. Beside it, one generated
        surface composes a broad dark plain, worn highlands and a broken pale
        junction from raster material regions. Both use the preferred 02.10 C
        light field, including the original shadow depth and neutral colour. The
        02.11 tuning candidates remain separate, so a lighting change cannot
        disguise a material difference.
      </p>
      <RockLight030 round="transfer" client:load />
      <p>
        Matched production Sun, Wasp, World/LFX stack and transforms: radius 45,
        centre distance 220, 0.6 px/u and 54-pixel bodies; lower details are 81
        pixels. The candidate is a deterministic 192-pixel Canvas raster
        computed once on mount, with broad authored regions, noise-broken
        boundaries and restrained local relief. It samples no image, adds no
        crater symbols and carries no broad painted illumination. No new image
        was generated for this round.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a transfer specimen, not a replacement for the accepted visual
        target. Its material remains study-local until its character earns a
        shared implementation. It covers one projected hemisphere, without a
        planet-family recipe, rotation, wrapping or transient-light overlap
        tests. Cached drawing is demonstrated; production frame cost is not
        measured. A flatter or less corporeal result would identify a
        material-generation gap, not reopen the preferred art direction.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Finding:</strong>
        the original image still looks better, but the difference is small
        enough to move on from fidelity tuning. The generated specimen is
        sufficiently close for studying variety. Further crispness can be
        revisited if needed; matching the reference’s finest detail is not the
        current priority. This does not settle a final material recipe.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-13">
        02.13 / Different worlds from the same material method
      </summary>
      <p>
        Three compositions—mineral-rich stone, basalt with highlands and scarred
        ironstone—each use the same seeds 71, 97 and 153. Seed 71 is left, 97 is
        above and 153 is right; lower views repeat them at 81 pixels. All scenes
        use radius 45, centre distance 220, 0.6 px/u and the neutral 02.10 C LFX
        treatment. The examples remain available as evidence of the material
        gap.
      </p>
      <RockLight030 round="family" client:load />
      <p>
        The mineral and scarred-ironstone proposals do not work as game planets;
        their soft, mottled surfaces suggest candy popcorn rather than rock.
        Basalt and highlands remains the more useful specimen, but the family
        test shows that the procedural transfer is not yet convincing. The image
        references retain stronger crisp features and local contrast. A lack of
